The Task 2 reviewer approved the hard parts — it independently verified the deferral against hyper's actual dispatch loop rather than trusting the report — but raised one **Important** finding it explicitly labeled *plan-mandated*. That's your call, not mine.

**The finding.** Test 3, `gate_withholds_later_chunks_until_released`, is the only test whose pass is timing-dependent, and it's one-directional: `assert!(!handle.is_finished())` after a 200 ms sleep means that on any machine where a loopback round trip exceeds 200 ms, **a completely broken gate passes**. The trailing full-body `assert_eq!` doesn't rescue it, because a broken gate produces byte-identical output.

Worse, it never asserts the pre-gate chunk actually *reached the client*. That's the property Tasks 9 and 11 build their cancellation scenarios on — if it silently breaks, those scenarios fire cancellation before the client has seen a single byte and pass vacuously, with all four tests green.

**The plan text that mandates it** — my own, in Task 2 Step 1:

```rust
// The second chunk must still be withheld.
tokio::time::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_millis(200)).await;
assert!(!handle.is_finished(), "body completed before the gate released");
```

This is the same vacuity class I made the implementer fix in test 4 an hour ago. I wrote the weaker form into the plan, and the reviewer is right that the plan doesn't get to grade itself out of it.

